The `20230706_124619.jpg` residual DIFF
---------------------------------------

This file is a *baseline* JPEG (file(1): "baseline, precision 8")
with DRI restart markers, NOT a progressive JPEG. Its 3 DIFF bytes
sit at offsets 1129740 / 2431078 / 3344392, each immediately before
an RST marker (0xFFD0/D1/D2). The source has `0x00` as the entropy-
stream padding byte; our reconstruction emits `0x7f` (spec-correct
upper-bits padding).

This is the `has_zero_padding_bit` / `padding_bits` JBRD signal,
which lives on a different axis from the task-scoped
`extra_zero_runs` + `reset_points`. The marker scanner in
`jxl-encoder/src/jpeg/parse.rs` currently always sets
`has_zero_padding_bit: false` and never populates `padding_bits`.
Fixing this requires tracking the *exact bit padding* per RST segment
in zenjpeg's entropy decoder (every restart segment's final byte
needs to be replayed verbatim, including any non-spec zero-bit
padding the original encoder emitted).

This is a clean follow-on chunk and explicitly *not* part of task #10.
The original task meta in `jpeg_in_jxl_recompression_2026-05-28.meta`
mis-characterised this file as having "successive approximation (Ah>0)
AC refinement scans" — that was incorrect; the actual root cause for
this one file is the entropy padding-bit issue.

API design
----------

Approach (B from the task spec): keep `decode_coefficients()` signature
unchanged; add a NEW method `decode_coefficients_with_jbrd_metadata()`
returning an EXTENDED tuple `(DecodedCoefficients, JbrdMetadata)`.

  - `DecodedCoefficients` struct fields untouched — byte-for-byte
    back-compat for every existing caller (zenjpeg-bench-utils, zjpeg,
    zenmetrics, codec-corpus, codec-eval, etc.).
  - `JbrdMetadata` is a new public type, additively re-exported from
    `zenjpeg::decoder::{JbrdMetadata, JbrdScanInfo}`.
  - Internal: `EntropyDecoder` gains two new public tracking methods
    (`decode_ac_first_scan_tracked` / `decode_ac_refine_scan_tracked`)
    that take an `Option<&mut JbrdScanInfo>` parameter. The legacy
    methods (`decode_ac_first_scan` / `decode_ac_refine_scan`) are
    now thin shims that delegate with `None`, so any external caller
    of those `pub` methods is byte-identical.
  - `JpegParser` gains an internal `jbrd_scans: Option<Vec<JbrdScanInfo>>`
    field, default `None`, enabled via the new `enable_jbrd_tracking()`
    private helper.

Cost
----

When `decode_coefficients()` is used, the tracking path is fully off
(`jbrd: Option = None`). The inner AC scan loops gain one `is_some()`
check at the top (constant cost, branch-predictable) and zero
additional work per block. Existing benches show no measurable
overhead.

When `decode_coefficients_with_jbrd_metadata()` is used, the cost is
one branch per block on the EOB / single-EOB / EOB-run path, plus
one `Vec::push` whenever a JBRD signal actually fires (rare per
block but accumulates across the image). Encoded-bytes equivalent
to existing decode + a fraction of a percent of decode wall time.

Reproducibility
---------------

  - Build: see "Build" above.
  - Encode: same `cjxl-rs <src> <jxl> -e 5 -d 0` invocations.
  - Decode: `djxl <jxl> <jpg>`.
  - Verify: `cmp <src.jpg> <jpg>`.

Bench script: small inline Python (see task #10 commit message in
this repo).

DO NOT
------

  - DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte movement (per W44-66).
    The new bytes that move are JBRD bitstream-reconstruction bytes,
    not FP-numeric.
  - DO NOT default-flip the tracking in `decode_coefficients()`.
    That entry point's zero-overhead contract is load-bearing for
    benches and pipelines that call it in tight loops.
  - DO NOT publish zenjpeg 0.8.5 to crates.io until the user signs
    off. The version bump is for local workspace consumption only;
    jxl-encoder path-pins the sibling worktree explicitly.
